## The Face-Up Illusion

The Face-Up Illusion: an emerald cut looks longer than a round brilliant of the same carat weight, but it covers less total finger area. Both statements are true at the same time.

A 1ct round brilliant is 6.5mm in diameter — a circle. A [1ct emerald cut](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=0.9&caratMax=1.1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is approximately 6.5mm long and 4.5mm wide — a rectangle. The lengths match.

The width is 31% narrower. The emerald cut is longer in one direction and shorter in the other.

This creates a directional illusion. Looking at the ring straight-on from above, the emerald cut looks longer and more impressive than a round. Looking at it from the side or when worn naturally on the hand, the width differential becomes visible.

The emerald cut's narrower footprint can make it appear "smaller" on some finger widths — even though it's the same carat weight as a round that looks "bigger."

Neither impression is wrong. The Face-Up Illusion is a geometry fact about rectangular vs circular shapes. A rectangle and a circle with the same diagonal dimension always give different visual impressions.

What buyers need to know: compare your emerald cut to other emerald cuts by L×W dimensions, not just carat weight. Compare it to rounds by length only (not by diameter), because the shapes don't have equivalent "sizes" in the same sense.

---

## The Depth Penalty

The Depth Penalty: any carat weight hidden in the depth of an emerald cut is carat weight you cannot see face-up.

An emerald cut is a three-dimensional object. The carat weight is the total mass of the stone. That mass is distributed between the crown (visible table and step-cut facets above the girdle), the girdle, and the pavilion (everything below the girdle, hidden inside the setting).

A stone cut with a deep pavilion hides more of its carat weight below the girdle where you cannot see it.

Two 1ct emerald cuts can look like different sizes. If one is cut at 60% total depth and the other at 67% depth, the shallower stone has more of its weight distributed in the crown and girdle — which means a larger face-up spread. At 1 carat, a depth of 60% gives roughly 6.7mm × 4.7mm face-up.

At 1 carat and 67% depth, the same carat weight gives a significantly smaller face-up — closer to 6.2mm × 4.3mm.

> **Farzana says:** "When you're comparing two 1ct emerald cuts and one is priced much lower, check the depth percentage first. A stone at 67–68% depth is hiding carat weight you can't see. The same carat at 60–62% looks noticeably bigger on the hand. Buyers overpay for depth all the time without knowing it."

The Depth Penalty applies to every shape, but it hits the emerald cut harder than brilliant cuts. A round brilliant's facets are designed to compensate for depth through sparkle. An emerald cut's step-cut facets create a large, flat table — and a shallower table means more visible face-up area.

Depth matters more here.

**Target depth range for emerald cuts:** 58–64%. Below 58% and the stone starts to look glassy and shallow. Above 65% and The Depth Penalty becomes significant.

---

## The L:W Rule

The L:W Rule: the length-to-width ratio of an emerald cut controls how the stone looks on your finger more than any single dimension.

An emerald cut at 1.25:1 L:W is almost square — it looks compact and wide. An emerald cut at 1.50:1 L:W is distinctly elongated — it looks long and slim. An emerald cut at 1.35:1 L:W is the classic midpoint, elongated but not extreme.

The same carat weight in each ratio creates a completely different ring presence on the hand.

> **Farzana says:** "L:W ratio is the most underrated decision in choosing an emerald cut. Two 2ct emerald cuts — one at 1.25:1 and one at 1.50:1 — look like completely different stones on the same finger. The higher ratio elongates the finger and looks more dramatically elongated in a photograph. The lower ratio looks squarer and more substantial but less elongated. Neither is better. They're different preferences."

The most common L:W ratio for emerald cuts is between 1.30:1 and 1.50:1. Below 1.30:1 looks too square to most buyers. Above 1.55:1 starts to look stretched, which some buyers specifically want (see [elongated emerald cut diamonds](/elongated-emerald-cut-diamond)).

The chart above uses approximately 1.35:1 as the standard.

A wider stone (lower L:W) at the same carat weight will be shorter in length but wider. A narrower stone (higher L:W) at the same carat weight will be longer in length but narrower in width. The total face-up area is similar, but the shape impression is different.

