## How Size Changes Per Carat Weight Jump

The size increase per carat is not linear. Doubling the carat weight does not double the face-up dimensions. Here is the actual size increase at each common carat jump:

<table class="dc-custom-table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Carat Jump</th>
      <th>Length Change</th>
      <th>Width Change</th>
      <th>Perception</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>0.50ct → 1.00ct (+100% weight)</td>
      <td>+1.5mm (5.0 → 6.5mm)</td>
      <td>+1.0mm (3.5 → 4.5mm)</td>
      <td>Clearly visible — a major jump</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>1.00ct → 1.50ct (+50% weight)</td>
      <td>+1.0mm (6.5 → 7.5mm)</td>
      <td>+1.0mm (4.5 → 5.5mm)</td>
      <td>Clearly visible — most noticeable per dollar</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>1.50ct → 2.00ct (+33% weight)</td>
      <td>+1.0mm (7.5 → 8.5mm)</td>
      <td>+0.5mm (5.5 → 6.0mm)</td>
      <td>Visible but smaller than the 1–1.5ct jump</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>2.00ct → 2.50ct (+25% weight)</td>
      <td>+0.5mm (8.5 → 9.0mm)</td>
      <td>+0.5mm (6.0 → 6.5mm)</td>
      <td>Subtle — side-by-side comparison needed</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>2.50ct → 3.00ct (+20% weight)</td>
      <td>+0.5mm (9.0 → 9.5mm)</td>
      <td>+0.5mm (6.5 → 7.0mm)</td>
      <td>Subtle — most noticeable in photos</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>3.00ct → 4.00ct (+33% weight)</td>
      <td>+1.0mm (9.5 → 10.5mm)</td>
      <td>+0.8mm (7.0 → 7.8mm)</td>
      <td>Clearly visible at this scale</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>4.00ct → 5.00ct (+25% weight)</td>
      <td>+1.0mm (10.5 → 11.5mm)</td>
      <td>+0.7mm (7.8 → 8.5mm)</td>
      <td>Clearly visible at this scale</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

The 1.0ct to 1.5ct size jump delivers the most visible face-up improvement per additional dollar spent — both dimensions increase by approximately 1mm each. The 2.0ct to 2.5ct and 2.5ct to 3.0ct jumps add only 0.5mm per dimension and require 25–33% more carat weight (and budget) to achieve.

---

## How Finger Size Affects How Big an Emerald Cut Looks

The same stone looks larger on a smaller finger. A 2ct emerald cut at 8.5mm × 6.0mm on a size 5 finger (approximately 15.7mm diameter) covers more than half the finger width. On a size 8 finger (approximately 18.2mm diameter), the same stone covers about 47% of the finger width.

This is not just a visual trick — it's a real difference in proportion. Ring buyers with smaller fingers (size 4–5.5) often find that a 1.5ct emerald cut looks as impressive as a 2ct on a larger finger. Buyers with larger fingers (size 7+) may want to add 0.25–0.50ct to their target carat weight to achieve the same visual proportion.

The emerald cut's elongated shape is particularly flattering on hands with shorter fingers. The length of the stone visually extends the finger. A round brilliant of the same carat weight fills width but does not create the same lengthening illusion.

---

## Expert Summary

The emerald cut diamond size comparison is a three-variable problem: carat weight sets the mass, depth percentage controls how much of that mass is visible face-up, and L:W ratio controls the shape of the visible area.

The Face-Up Illusion explains why emerald cuts look longer than rounds of the same weight. The Depth Penalty explains why two stones of the same carat weight can show meaningfully different face-up dimensions. The L:W Rule explains why ratio preference is as important as carat weight in determining how the stone looks on the hand.

For most buyers, the key numbers are: [1ct = 6.5 × 4.5mm](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=0.9&caratMax=1.1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational), [2ct = 8.5 × 6.0mm](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=1.9&caratMax=2.1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational), [3ct = 9.5 × 7.0mm](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=2.9&caratMax=3.1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). These are well-proportioned stone targets. Any stone significantly outside these dimensions at the stated carat weight should be examined for excess depth before buying.

> **Farzana says:** "Before you buy any emerald cut, get the length, width, and depth in millimeters from the listing. Then divide the depth by the width to get the depth percentage. If it's over 65%, the stone is hiding weight from you. If it's under 58%, the stone may be cut too shallow. The table in this guide tells you exactly what face-up size to expect at each carat weight."

---

## Best For

**Buyers comparing EC to round:** Start with The Face-Up Illusion. A [1ct emerald cut](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=0.9&caratMax=1.1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is longer than a 1ct round but narrower. Neither is "bigger" — they're dimensionally different.

**Buyers hunting maximum face-up size per dollar:** Target depth percentage 60–62% and L:W ratio 1.35–1.45:1. Avoid any stone with depth over 65%. [Browse by those parameters](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and filter by dimensions, not just carat weight.

**Buyers looking up a specific stone's carat weight:** Use the MM-to-Carat reverse lookup table above. Input the seller's stated dimensions into the table for a carat weight estimate, then verify against the grading report.

**Buyers choosing between carat weights:** The 1ct to 1.5ct jump delivers the best visible size increase per dollar. The [1.5ct at 7.5 × 5.5mm](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=1.4&caratMax=1.6&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) vs the [1ct at 6.5 × 4.5mm](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=emerald&caratMin=0.9&caratMax=1.1&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the highest-impact carat jump for the money.

